<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1153">1153</a>–<a href="/years/1189">1189</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William the Good</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II, called the Good, was the Norman King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1166 to 1189. His fleets campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1185, and supported Isaac Komnenos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. He built the cathedral of Monreale, and his childless death opened the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily.jpg" alt="William II of Sicily" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dedication_mosaic_-_Cathedral_of_Monreale_-_Italy_2015_(crop).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>William was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> to King William I and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. His father died when he was twelve, and the government came under his mother's regency, run first by the chancellor Stephen du Perche until a coup in 1168, then by Walter Ophamil, archbishop of Palermo, and Matthew of Ajello. William was declared adult in 1171. The sources leave his character indistinct: he is described as secluded and pleasure-loving, seldom leaving his palace, yet his reign had an ambitious foreign policy. He championed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and was in secret league with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>.</p><p>His marriage in February 1177 to Joan, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>, marked his high position in European politics. He had refused Barbarossa's offer of his daughter Beatrice in 1173, so as not to offend the pope, and in July 1177 he sent a delegation to sign the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> with the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>William first attacked Ayyubid Egypt, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> threatened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. In July 1174 a force of 30,000 men landed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, but Saladin's arrival made the Sicilians re-embark in disorder. After the death of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel Comnenus in 1180 he turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Dyrrhachium was captured on 11 June 1185, and the fleet took Corfu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, which fell in August 1185 and was sacked.</p><p>Troops marching on the capital were defeated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> on 7 November 1185 by the army of Emperor Isaac Angelus under Alexios Branas. In 1189 William made peace with Isaac, giving up all the conquests. His admiral Margarito kept the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> open for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> to retire from Tripoli in the spring of 1188.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1184 William released his aunt Constance from her convent and in January 1186 married her to Henry, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, causing an oath to be taken to her as heir presumptive. William died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in November 1189, leaving no surviving children. Norman officials led by Matthew of Ajello then supported his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-king-of-sicily">Tancred of Lecce</a>, rather than Constance, to avoid German rule. The nickname the Good arose only decades after his death, from the end of the troubles of his father's reign and the wars under his successor. Dante places him in Paradise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William II of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1185 the Norman admiral Margaritus of Brindisi took Cephalonia and Zakynthos from Byzantium for <b>William II of Sicily</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-cephalonia-and-zakynthos">County Palatine of Cephalonia and Zakynthos</a></li><li><blockquote>He secured his position by allying with <b>William II of Sicily</b>, whose fleet defeated a Byzantine expedition sent by Isaac II Angelos in 1186-1187, and reportedly with Saladin.</blockquote><a href="/state/cyprus-under-isaac-komnenos">Cyprus under Isaac Komnenos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.12,13.36&amp;year=1176" aria-label="Palermo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 794 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William II of Sicily"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5333 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.12,13.36&amp;year=1176">What was here in 1176? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
